  <event id="40">
      <start>14:00</start>
      <duration>01:00</duration>
      <room>AW1.124</room>
      <slug></slug>
      <title>coreboot and PC technical details</title>
      <subtitle></subtitle>
      <track>Coreboot</track>
      <type></type>
      <language></language>
      <abstract>&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A modern PC is quite different from the 1980s original, and while the BIOS still lingers after 30 years it must now solve many tricky problems.&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;  </abstract>
      <description>&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When the original PC with it&amp;#39;s pre-ISA expansion bus was powered on, it was almost immediately ready to run an application. Today&amp;#39;s PC can have several multicore CPUs interconnected by HyperTransport, Front Side Bus or QuickPath, DDR3 RAM on each CPU, and a large number of buses and peripherals. Many components require increasingly complex initialization to be implemented in software. This talk describes the technical challenges encountered by coreboot developers and their solutions.&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;  </description>
      <persons>
          <person id="401">Peter Stuge</person>
      </persons>
      <links></links>
      <video></video>
      <end></end>
  </event>